                    <h1 class="text-[32px] md:text-[44px] font-extrabold tracking-tight leading-[0.95]">Rent Reporting: Does Paying Rent Boost Credit Score? +12 Points Explained</h1>
                    <p class="mt-4 text-[16px] md:text-[18px] leading-7 text-slate-500">Your rent is likely your biggest monthly bill — but it usually doesn't help your credit unless you report it. Here's how rent reporting works, which US services actually report, and who sees +10 to +20 points.</p>

                    <!-- Author Box -->
                    <div class="mt-8 p-4 rounded-2xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 flex gap-4 items-start">
                        <div class="w-11 h-11 rounded-full bg-slate-900 text-white flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-[13px]">AS</div>
                        <div class="flex-1">
                            <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 items-center">
                                <span class="font-bold text-[14px]">Written by Amanda S.</span><span class="text-[12px] px-2 py-0.5 rounded-full bg-white border">Former CFPB Analyst</span>
                            </div>
                            <div class="mt-1 text-[13px] text-slate-500 leading-6">Amanda spent 4 years analyzing consumer complaints at the CFPB. She focuses on credit reporting accuracy and renter financial health. This guide cites CFPB, Experian, Equifax, TransUnion.</div>
                            <div class="mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-slate-200/60 flex gap-3 items-center">
                                <div class="w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-emerald-500 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-[11px] font-bold">JL</div>
                                <span class="text-[12px]"><b>Reviewed by Dr James L., CFA</b> — Credit risk methodology review, Oct 20, 2026</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

