                    <div class="prose mt-10">
                        <div class="rounded-2xl bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-100 p-5 mb-8">
                            <h3 class="!mt-0">Key Takeaway</h3>
                            <p class="!mb-0"><b>Does paying rent build credit? Not automatically.</b> In the US, landlords rarely report to Experian, Equifax, TransUnion unless you use a rent reporting service. With 24 months of retroactive reporting, thin files can gain +10 to +20 points per TransUnion data — but late payments can also hurt.</p>
                        </div>

                        <h2>1. Why Rent Doesn't Build Credit By Default</h2>
                        <p>Unlike a credit card or auto loan, rent is not considered a credit account under federal reporting rules. According to the <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-credit-report-en-309/" class="underline">CFPB</a>, most property managers do not furnish rent data to the three major bureaus. That means 12 months of on-time $1,800 payments may not show up at all.</p>
                        <p><b>Experian clarifies</b> that rent appears only if: (a) your property manager reports directly, or (b) you use a third-party rent reporting service that reports to Experian RentBureau, or (c) collection occurs. Source: Experian.com/creditscore. <b>Equifax</b> notes similar — rent tradelines are optional furnishers.</p>

                        <h2>2. Which Rent Reporting Services Are Legit in the US?</h2>
                        <p>We reviewed 4 major US services that report rent without requiring you to change how you pay. All work with apartments, single-family, and roommate situations across all 50 states.</p>

                        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose my-6">
                            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-5">
                                <div class="font-bold">Boom (formerly LevelCredit)</div>
                                <div class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Reports to:</b> TransUnion, Equifax, Experian</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Cost:</b> $3.29/mo or $39/yr, includes 24-month back-reporting</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Best for:</b> Max bureau coverage, FICO 10 + VantageScore</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-5">
                                <div class="font-bold">RentTrack</div>
                                <div class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Reports to:</b> TransUnion, Equifax, Experian</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Cost:</b> $6.95-$10.95/mo, often paid by landlord</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Best for:</b> Credit-building residents in RentTrack partnered properties</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-5">
                                <div class="font-bold">Piñata (Pinata)</div>
                                <div class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Reports to:</b> TransUnion (primary), up to 24mo history</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Cost:</b> $2.99/mo free rewards tier + cashback offers</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Best for:</b> Lowest cost entry, renters who want perks</div>
                            </div>
                            <div class="rounded-xl border bg-white p-5">
                                <div class="font-bold">Esusu</div>
                                <div class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Reports to:</b> Experian, TransUnion, Equifax</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Cost:</b> Free if landlord partnership; otherwise $0-$10 via Esusu platform</div>
                                <div class="text-[13px] text-slate-500"><b>Best for:</b> Affordable housing portfolios, no-fee when covered</div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p class="text-[12px] text-slate-400 border-l-2 border-slate-200 pl-3">Source comparison based on public pricing pages as of Oct 2026. Esusu's $0 model requires your building enrolled — check with property manager before enrolling.</p>

                        <h2>3. How Much Does Rent Reporting Improve Credit? TransUnion Data</h2>
                        <p>TransUnion's renter credit study analyzed 12,000+ thin-file consumers (≤2 tradelines). Findings:</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><b>+10 to +20 points average</b> for renters added to VantageScore after 6 months of on-time rent reporting. Some saw +30 with 24-month back-reporting.</li>
                            <li><b>Thin files benefit most:</b> If you have no credit or score <600, rent can help you generate a VantageScore 3.0 first, then FICO 10.</li>
                            <li><b>FICO 10T counts rent:</b> Newer FICO 10T includes rent in trended data when reported as tradeline, not just alternative data. FICO 8 may ignore rent unless bureau shows it as open account.</li>
                            <li><b>Negative impact possible:</b> Late rent reported as 30+ days delinquent lowers score similar to other accounts. Always pay on time.</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p>Experian Boost and UltraFICO are different — they scan bank payments. Dedicated rent services report a true rent tradeline which lenders see on your file, unlike Boost which some mortgage underwriters discount.</p>

                        <h2>4. Who Benefits Most From Rent Reporting?</h2>
                        <h3>Great fit if:</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li>No SSN needed to start? <b>Boom allows ITIN,</b> useful for newcomers — but bureaus need name + address + DOB match.</li>
                            <li>You pay $800+ on time for 12+ months but score stuck due to no credit mix</li>
                            <li>Rent is your only installment/rent tradeline — adds housing history lenders like for mortgage</li>
                            <li>Building from 0: Combine rent + secured card + Self Credit Builder</li>
                        </ul>
                        <h3>Maybe skip if:</h3>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You have frequent late payments — reporting makes damage permanent on report</li>
                            <li>You already have 740+ FICO with strong mix — lift likely 0-5 points</li>
                            <li>Your landlord already reports for free — don't pay duplicate</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h2>5. How to Start Rent Reporting in 4 Steps (US)</h2>
                        <p><b>Time:</b> 10 minutes. No SSN required to create some accounts (Piñata, Boom accept ITIN), but SSN/ITIN needed for bureaus to match.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li><b>Step 1 — Confirm landlord doesn't report.</b> Ask management: "Do you report to TransUnion, Experian, Equifax via RentBureau?" 80% say no per CFPB.</li>
                            <li><b>Step 2 — Pick service.</b> If you want cheapest, Piñata $2.99. If you want max boost, Boom $3.29 all bureaus. If apartment is Esusu partner, use free Esusu first.</li>
                            <li><b>Step 3 — Upload lease + payment proof.</b> Services verify via bank linking (Plaid) or portal upload. Allow 2-year back-reporting — this is key for extra points.</li>
                            <li><b>Step 4 — Monitor reports.</b> Check 45 days later in AnnualCreditReport.com (only official free source). Look under "Open Accounts" or "Other" for rent tradeline. ScoreUp calculators can simulate +12 impact.</li>
                        </ul>

                        <h2>6. Cost vs Benefit: Is $2-$10/month Worth It?</h2>
                        <p>At $2.99-$10.95/month ($36-$131/year), rent reporting is cheaper than most credit builder loans. If you gain even +12 points and move from 580→600+ you may qualify for better cards — saving more than fee. However, if service reports to only one bureau, mortgage lenders using FICO 2,4,5 may not see it.</p>
                        <p>Pro tip: Cancel after 12 months once you have 2+ revolving trade lines — rent history stays for up to 7 years but future reporting stops. Equifax says closed rent tradelines still age.</p>

